 *
 * aen-temp-sensor -- app overlay for the E1M-AEN801 (Alif Ensemble E8, M55-HE).
 *
 * THIS OVERLAY DELIBERATELY ADDS NOTHING, AND THAT IS THE POINT.
 *
 * The on-module TMP112 is a property of the SoM, not of this app: every
 * E1M-AEN801 has one, so every AEN801 application should get it for free from
 * the BOARD layer.  The board files declare both halves --
 *
 *   - BRD_I2C, the on-module housekeeping bus.  It is SoC I2C0
 *     (i2c@49010000, "snps,designware-i2c") on pads P7_0 (I2C0_SDA_C) and
 *     P7_1 (I2C0_SCL_C), per the E1M-AEN-2626-R2 netlist rows
 *     "BRD_I2C_SDA",U6,B8,"P7_0/SCP0_A" and "BRD_I2C_SCL",U6,B3,"P7_1/SCP1_A"
 *     and the AE822 DFP pin table's alt-function [4] entries.
 *   - the "ti,tmp112" child node on it, at the design address 0x40: the
 *     netlist ties U20 pin 3 (ADD0) to 0V, and U20 (TMP112DIDPWR) is the
 *     X2SON-5 "Address Variant Only" package -- per TI SBOS473L p.44 no
 *     SOT563-6 orderable carries that MPN -- whose SBOS473L Table 7-4 strap
 *     row maps ADD0 -> GND to 0x40.
 *
 * -- so an app overlay that re-declared them would be duplicating a SoM fact
 * one layer too high.  That duplication is exactly the smell that
 * examples/aen/aen-secure-element-sign and examples/aen/aen-brd-i2c-scan carry
 * today: each hand-rolls its own &pinctrl group and &i2c0 enable, and every
 * copy is a place the pad config can drift out of step with the netlist.
 *
 * The file exists at all because Zephyr auto-applies a board overlay only when
 * its stem matches the FULLY-QUALIFIED board id -- so the name here is the
 * contract, even when the contents are empty.  If you need an app-scoped
 * devicetree tweak (a different `conversion-rate`, say, or the ITCM retarget
 * for a Flow C RAM-run), this is where it goes.
 */
